@vsm 1.0
@status active
// attack_surface_expansion.spec.vsm
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-01.
// Registers a strategic arc from a single conversation, grounded in live investigation
// (not speculation) of bbh-lab's actual pipeline on LXC 111 — same day the C0 eligibility
// gate, the merkle_break root-cause fix, and the VLAN20/jupyter-lab isolation redesign
// all landed. Four gaps found, each verified against real code/output before being
// registered here, with a proposed build order — not built yet, this is the spec.

spec(BBH-ATTACK-SURFACE-EXPANSION) {

  premise: "rmw3, after C0 landed clean: 'ya tenemos un pipeline mas o menos limpio, que
      explora dentro de los margenes legales de cada uno de los programas.' Agreed, with
      the caveat this is a good BASELINE, not a finished state — rmw3: 'partamos verificando
      el historico y asi vamos matando los piojos de a uno.' This spec is the SECOND half of
      that same conversation: not what's broken in what exists, but what's genuinely
      UNCOVERED — real gaps found by reading the actual code, not assumed from the module
      names. Four gaps, roughly in the order they surfaced, each independently verified live
      on LXC 111 before being written down here.",

  // ── GAP 1: browser exists, unused for vulnerability testing ──────────────────────
  gap_1_dom_client_side: {
    finding: "Confirmed LIVE (bypassing the shell-profile's stale HTTP_PROXY env var,
        which caused a false 'browser doesn't work' timeout on first attempt — root cause
        was proxy wiring, not capability): bbh-lab has a REAL, WORKING headless Chromium on
        LXC 111 itself (Python playwright + a configured playwright-mcp), no desktop
        environment needed. Confirmed it already gets used for 3 things: policy-page
        capture (bbh_guidelines_fetcher.py), credential capture/validation
        (bbh_creds_capture.py, bbh_credentials.py), and session bridging
        (bbh_session_bridge.py, has a 'playwright-inject' command). It has NEVER been
        pointed at actual vulnerability testing. All ~25+ existing phase-4 vuln modules
        (4_vuln.py's nuclei-template battery + the ~15 specialized 4x_*.py modules) operate
        purely on HTTP request/response patterns — zero JS execution.",
    uncovered_classes: [
      "DOM-based XSS (sink only fires client-side; nuclei's static response-body matching
       cannot see it)",
      "postMessage handler vulnerabilities (cross-origin messaging misconfig)",
      "Client-side prototype pollution triggered via real page interaction (distinct from
       the server-side check 4s_prototype_poll.py already does over HTTP)",
      "Clickjacking VALIDATED by actually rendering in a real iframe (today: only a header
       presence check via nuclei, X-Frame-Options/CSP frame-ancestors, never confirmed by
       attempting the frame)",
      "Client-side routing / SPA auth-bypass (routing logic lives in JS, invisible to
       server-side response inspection)",
      "CSP bypass verified by real execution vs. just reading the declared policy",
    ],
    why_it_matters: "This is the class of vulnerability the shift to modern JS-heavy SPAs
        (which most of the current program portfolio — stripe, airtable, gitlab, github —
        genuinely is) makes MORE common over time, not less. A pipeline that is
        HTTP-request-only is structurally blind to a growing share of real bugs, and the
        capability to fix this already exists, unused.",
  },

  // ── GAP 2: business-value endpoint scoring doesn't exist ─────────────────────────
  gap_2_business_value_scoring: {
    finding: "Confirmed LIVE: 2d_classify.py's tiering (tier1/2/3 + category: api, auth,
        admin, ssrf, graphql, params, javascript, data, form, dynamic, static, tracking,
        font, cdn) is entirely TECHNICAL — what KIND of endpoint, never how much it matters
        to the specific business. rmw3's original framing: 'identificar endpoints que
        tengan mayor valor de investigar... conociendo valor comercial, estrategico,
        comunicacional... dependiendo su linea de negocio.'",
    proposed_signal: "Do not invent a generic heuristic for 'business value' — the
        authoritative signal already exists and is already captured: each program's OWN
        severity guidelines (the same text C0's ineligible_findings comes from) explicitly
        name what THAT business considers critical. Concrete example already verified
        today: github's severity guide names 'bypassing the login process' and 'access to
        another user's data in GitHub Actions' as Critical, in the program's own words.
        Extract the entity/flow types a program's OWN severity text names as
        high/critical (not just the ineligible list — the POSITIVE severity examples too),
        and weight 2d_classify.py's existing technical tiers against that per-program
        signal instead of a fixed generic ranking.",
    why_it_matters: "Directly serves the efficiency goal rmw3 named ('busqueda mas
        eficiente... pero principalmente que pueda entregar mejores resultados') — testing
        effort gets weighted toward what a program has ALREADY told us it will pay most
        for, using data the pipeline already has, not new capture.",
  },

  // ── GAP 3: perimeter/WAF layer — detected, never engaged ─────────────────────────
  gap_3_waf_perimeter: {
    finding: "Confirmed LIVE: bbh_waf_detect.py exists, is wired into 4_vuln.py, and does
        EXACTLY ONE thing — detect WAF presence + type, write it to waf_detection.json.
        Zero matches (grepped explicitly) for bypass/evasion/evade/encode-payload/obfuscate
        logic anywhere in the file or its callers. This directly explains a real pattern
        already seen in TODAY's batch-1 report: 'GF hits altos (xss 4617, ssrf 4665, sqli
        598) pero 0 validados (WAF/falsos positivos)' — the pipeline hits the WAF and
        walks away; whatever is behind it is never tested.",
    also_uncovered: "Origin-IP discovery (finding the real backend server behind a
        CDN/WAF to route around the front-end protection entirely) — a well-known,
        long-standing technique, not found anywhere in the current module set.",
    guardrail_before_build: "WAF bypass sits closer to the aggressive end of testing than
        anything currently automated. Per the C0 discipline established earlier the same
        day (verify policy BEFORE building, not after): check each program's already-
        captured policy text for language about evasion/obfuscation/circumventing security
        controls specifically, before writing any bypass module. This may be PASS for some
        programs and explicitly prohibited for others — do not assume universal green
        light. Detection can stay universal; bypass execution should be gated per-program
        like everything else, via the existing check_policy()/C0 machinery.",
  },

  // ── GAP 4: "detects but doesn't prove" — a recurring pattern, not one gap ────────
  gap_4_detect_without_proof: {
    finding: "A pattern found in 2 concrete places, confirmed live by reading the actual
        code (not assumed from naming): cve_lookup.py does version-match against NVD +
        CISA-KEV/EPSS scoring ONLY — zero exploit/PoC verification logic (grepped
        explicitly, no matches). 4i_token_hunter.py pattern-matches for secret-shaped
        strings — zero liveness validation (no matching function found). Both produce
        CANDIDATES, not reportable findings, for programs whose own severity guide demands
        demonstrated impact (github's own text, verified earlier today: 'must demonstrate
        an actual impact of exploitation... not basta version vieja' for known-vulnerable-
        software claims specifically).",
    proven_counter_example: "This is a SOLVABLE gap, not a structural limit — the pipeline
        already does this correctly elsewhere. interactsh_manager.py (wired into 4_vuln.py,
        referenced by bbh_stride.py too) gives blind SSRF/XXE a REAL out-of-band callback
        proof of actual exploitation, not just a plausible-looking signal. That is the
        pattern to replicate, not invent fresh: for CVEs with a known low-risk public PoC,
        run it; for found tokens/keys, make one minimal non-destructive read-only call
        against the API the key belongs to, before either candidate is treated as a
        reportable finding rather than a lead.",
  },

  // ── PROPOSED BUILD ORDER (reasoned, not just listed) ─────────────────────────────
  proposed_sequencing: {
    order_1_detect_without_proof: "HIGHEST priority. Smallest, most mechanical of the four
        — extends an ALREADY-PROVEN pattern (interactsh) to 2 more places. Directly
        increases the YIELD of the pipeline that already exists (more of what's already
        detected becomes reportable) without opening any new scope/policy questions — CVE
        version-checking and reading found keys are already within tested surface. Lowest
        risk, fastest payoff.",
    order_2_business_value_scoring: "SECOND. Also low-risk (a ranking/prioritization
        change, not a new testing capability) and mostly a text-extraction problem against
        data already captured for 8 programs. Directly serves the efficiency goal named at
        the start of this whole conversation.",
    order_3_dom_client_side: "THIRD. Larger, genuinely new capability (real testing
        modules using Playwright, not just detection) — high value because it is an
        entirely uncovered vulnerability CLASS, but more implementation effort and needs
        its own validation-gate design (how does a DOM-XSS PoC get certified, same rigor
        as everything else this project has built today).",
    order_4_waf_perimeter: "FOURTH, and gated: highest potential yield (unlocks findings
        currently discarded as WAF-blocked) but also the one requiring the guardrail above
        — per-program policy verification BEFORE any bypass logic gets written, not a
        universal capability rolled out at once.",
  },

  status: "SPEC ONLY — nothing in this document has been dispatched or built. Awaiting
      rmw3 review/sign-off on the sequencing before any of the four gaps gets a real
      directive to bbh-lab. Sits behind BBH-HISTORICAL-FINDINGS-VALIDATION in the current
      queue (TASKS.vsm) — that campaign is still running.",
}
